  "verbalization": "Arisugacin G appears to have a shared target of SNU-387 across multiple paths, with each of these targets ultimately linking to LAPC4 through a series of molecular interactions. The evidence suggests that Arisugacin G may act as a broad inhibitor of a class of compounds that are targets of SNU-387, which in turn interacts with LAPC4. This implies a potential mechanism where Arisugacin G may modulate the activity of a metabolic pathway or enzyme that is central to the function of SNU-387, which then affects LAPC4. The consistent presence of SNU-387 as a common target across all three paths supports the idea that Arisugacin G may influence a shared biological process, possibly through a common intermediate or pathway. The evidence does not provide direct evidence of a direct interaction between Arisugacin G and LAPC4, but rather a chain of targets that ultimately leads to LAPC4, suggesting a possible indirect mechanism of action.",
